---
name: sdt-toolbox
description: >
  Use this skill to choose the right tool for local files and the SDT corpus:
  reading a file or a line range, searching a tree or one file, listing a
  directory, comparing two files, appending to a document, or capturing a web
  page — even when the request does not name sdt. It states the boundary
  between sdt and the host tools, names the verb for each operation, and says
  what to do when sdt cannot do the job.
---

The boundary: the corpus, document state, web capture and structured data
belong to `sdt`; code editing, git, builds and running processes belong to the
host. Pick the verb by the operation, not by the tool you already reach for.

| Need | Verb | Instead of |
|---|---|---|
| corpus, by meaning | `sdt context search <q>` | `grep` |
| corpus text | `sdt read -p <ref>` | `cat` |
| line range | `sdt read --lines a:b` | `sed -n` |
| numbered lines | `sdt read --numbered` | host reader |
| listing, sizes | `sdt ls --stat` | `ls -la` |
| paths by glob | `sdt find <glob>` | `find` |
| tree search | `sdt grep <p>` | `grep -r` |
| one file | `sdt grep --path <f>` | `grep -n` |
| web page | `sdt crawldown <url>` | fetcher |
| JSON, API | `sdt http` into `sdt q` | `curl`/`jq` |
| two files | `sdt diff <a> <b>` | `diff` |
| append to corpus | `sdt context edit --append` | `cat >>` |
| replace one literal | `sdt edit -p <f> --old/--new` | `python3`/`sed -i` |
| document state | `sdt context check/status/close` | none |

A flag is real only if `sdt schema --command <name>` prints it; `--help` is the
last resort, not the map (`context/instructions/cli.md` carries the compact
verb list, `context/instructions/reference.md` the discovery routes).

When `sdt` cannot do the job — a surgical edit of a source file, a listing with
sizes while `sdt ls` is not shipped, a flag that does not exist — name the verb
that fell short, then use the host tool and keep going. The rule is about
spending the fewest calls on the work, not about obeying prose: the corpus is
the one thing a host tool must never write.
